	abstract = {Flying Ad Hoc Networks (FANETs) are gradually being deployed for mission-oriented applications and time-critical applications, but their performance is often limited by high communication overhead, limited energy, and dynamic topology. This paper suggests an Energy-Efficient Narwhal Optimization-Based Cooperative Routing Protocol (NOA-CRP-SFANET) to face these challenges. The protocol controls the Narwhal Optimization Algorithm for cluster head selection and cooperative routing, confirming minimal consumption of energy, reduced routing overhead, and enhanced packet delivery. The proposed method is estimated against Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) and Hybrid Deep Reinforcement Learning (HDRL) approaches in simulation scenarios with 50-200 UAVs. Results validate that NOA-CRP-SFANET attains up to 15% lower energy consumption, reduced latency and higher throughput, maintaining a packet delivery ratio above 96%, indicating greater stability and productivity in dense FANET deployments. NOA-CRP-SFANET is highlighted as a robust solution for energy-efficient and reliable UAV network operations. © 2025 IEEE.},
