	abstract = {Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) have been able to find feasible solutions to many of the challenges faced by various industrial sectors today. Notably, these vehicles often operate as networks, enhancing their capability to execute missions that may be difficult for humans. With the increasing prevalence of UAVs across various sectors, there is an increase in demand for real-time data analysis and decision-making, despite several challenges. Specifically, when deployed in critical missions like disaster response to survey areas and make informed decisions, communication with the base station may be compromised in harsh, dynamic environments. Under such scenarios, there has been a growing recognition of the potential benefits of in-flight data offloading and processing among UAVs where they resort to offloading data to peers with superior computational abilities. This strategy of data offloading can mitigate operation latency, improve efficiency, optimize the utilization of the heterogeneous group by balancing the workload, and enhance resilience to communication disruptions. This paper considers a multi-hop data offloading strategy among UAVs flying in formation. The solution is derived using multi-agent Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) framework, which computes the proportion of data each UAV processes based on its computational and battery, and the fraction to be offloaded. A comparison of three DRL algorithms-Actor-Critic, TD-error based and with eligibility traces is presented to solve the offloading problem for a given environment considering various factors, including capacity, task size, residual energy, and the spatial positioning of the nodes.  © 2024 IEEE.},
