	abstract = {With the booming low-altitude economy, low-altitude unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) are widely used in many industries, fueling surging communication needs. Exploiting existing terrestrial base stations (BSs) solely fail to meet such demands; instead, integrating 5G BSs with other communication systems, such as wireless ad hoc networks, might satisfy the communication demands. To maximize the communication performance of such a multi-modal network for low-altitude UAVs, the handover between the two aforementioned networks is a critical issue. In this paper, we propose a deep reinforcement learning (DRL)-based dynamic handover approach to maximize the quality of service (QoS) of the UAV and minimize the average handover rate simultaneously. Specifically, to address the rapidly varying link quality and network load in low-altitude scenarios, a comprehensive strategy integrates received signal strength (RSS), available bit rate (ABR), round-trip time (RTT), UAV motion states, and network costs in the DRL modeling. Extensive simulations validate the effectiveness of the proposed approach in terms of the QoS and average handover rate, compared to three baselines. © 2025 IEEE.},
